# Environment variable names only — no real values or secrets.
#
# Copy to .env.local per app when a variable is actually needed, and never
# commit real values.

# --- apps/admin: Cloudflare Access (Phase 6) --------------------------------
#
# Required in any deployed environment. When both are set, the admin app
# verifies the `Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion` header against Cloudflare's JWKS
# and fails closed if verification fails. When either is missing, the admin
# app denies access rather than falling back to anything.
#
# Neither is a secret: the team domain is a public hostname and the AUD tag
# is a public identifier for the Access application. They are configuration,
# not credentials, which is why no Cloudflare API token appears here.
#
# CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=          # e.g. yourteam.cloudflareaccess.com
# CF_ACCESS_AUD=                  # the Access application's AUD tag

# --- apps/admin: local development identity (Phase 6) -----------------------
#
# Lets you use the admin UI locally without a Cloudflare Access session.
# Off by default. It activates ONLY when all three are true: the build is
# not a production build, ADMIN_DEV_AUTH is exactly "enabled", and the
# CF_ACCESS_* values above are absent. A production build ignores it
# entirely — see docs/DECISIONS.md.
#
# ADMIN_DEV_AUTH=enabled
# ADMIN_DEV_EMAIL=                # display label only; grants nothing

# --- Cloudflare D1 and R2: no environment variables, by design --------------
#
# Both are reached through Worker **bindings**, which carry no credentials at
# all. There is deliberately nothing to put here:
#
#   * D1 is resolved by `apps/admin/src/lib/db/binding.ts`.
#   * R2 is resolved by `apps/admin/src/lib/storage/binding.ts`.
#
# Four S3-style R2 placeholders (`R2_ACCOUNT_ID`, `R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID`,
# `R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, `R2_BUCKET_NAME`) were listed here from Phase 1A and
# have been removed. They belong to the S3 API, which this project does not
# use: the delivery architecture was chosen partly so that no long-lived
# access key exists anywhere. Leaving an unused credential placeholder in an
# example file is an invitation to populate it.
#
# `DATABASE_URL` was listed here too and is likewise unused — D1 is a binding,
# not a connection string. It is left in place only because removing it was
# outside this task's scope; nothing reads it.
# DATABASE_URL=                     # unused; D1 comes from a binding

# --- Reserved for future phases (not yet used by any code) ------------------

# Admin authentication is handled by Cloudflare Access (see above). There is
# deliberately no AUTH_SECRET or password/session configuration: this project
# stores no passwords and issues no cookies of its own.

# --- Contact form email notifications (optional) ----------------------------
#
# The contact form works without any of these: every message is written to D1
# and read in the admin inbox. Setting them adds an email copy on top, and a
# failure to send never fails the visitor's submission.
#
# Names only, never values. Creating the API key is a human action — see
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md.
#
# Cloudflare Workers cannot open raw TCP sockets, so SMTP (Gmail's included)
# is not available; a provider with an HTTPS API is the only shape that works.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY=                   # from resend.com; free tier is enough
# CONTACT_NOTIFY_TO=                # where notifications are delivered
# CONTACT_NOTIFY_FROM=              # a sender on a domain verified with Resend

